{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Section 9 of the Act outlines the powers of the Secretary to request information from any party to a transaction or holding under review or investigation. The Secretary may require reports, documents, or any other information, before, during, or after a transaction or holding under review or investigation, and may conduct investigations, hold hearings, administer oaths, examine witnesses, and subpoena witnesses and documents. Information submitted to the Secretary under this Act must be kept confidential, except in certain circumstances such as pursuant to an administrative or judicial proceeding, an Act of Congress, a request from a relevant committee of Congress, or if the Secretary determines that the release of such information is in the national interest of the United States. Section 10 outlines the enforcement mechanisms of the Act, including investigations of violations of the Act, the authority of officers and employees to inspect and seize items, require information, and administer oaths or affirmations, and the power of the Attorney General to bring actions against violators.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1567, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Section 11 of this document outlines penalties for violating the regulations and orders issued under this Act. It is unlawful to violate, attempt to violate, conspire to violate, or cause a violation of any regulation or order issued under this Act. Civil penalties can be imposed on a person for each violation, including a fine of up to $250,000 or twice the value of the transaction that is the basis of the violation, whichever is greater. Criminal penalties can also be imposed, including fines of up to $1,000,000 and imprisonment for up to 20 years for individuals, as well as forfeiture of any property used or intended to be used to commit or facilitate the violation.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1188, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': "It's not necessary to have an `__init__` method in every class. If a class doesn't need to set any instance variables during initialization or perform any other special setup, then it's perfectly fine to omit the `__init__` method.\n\nIn your `Game` class, you don't have any instance variables that need to be set during initialization, so it makes sense to omit the `__init__` method. You're using other methods to set the player variables and run the game loop, which is perfectly fine.\n\nIt's also worth noting that if you ever do need to add an `__init__` method to a class later on, you can always do so. There's no requirement that you have to define the `__init__` method at the beginning of the class definition.", 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1254, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'In this section, the authors present their algorithm for inferring AS relationships using BGP paths, a list of AS allocations to RIRs and organizations, and multiple sources of validation data. The algorithm uses two metrics of AS connectivity: the node degree, which is the number of neighbors an AS has, and the transit degree, which is the number of unique neighbors that appear on either side of an AS in adjacent links. The algorithm makes three assumptions: the existence of a clique of multiple large transit providers, that a provider will announce customer routes to its providers, and that the AS topology can be represented in a directed acyclic graph. The algorithm is composed of several steps: filtering and sanitizing AS paths, inferring the clique of ASes at the top of the hierarchy, applying heuristics to identify c2p links, and classifying all remaining unclassified links as p2p.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1900, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Here\'s a revised version of the `findPerformersTagsbyID` function that returns a list of tags for a performer:\n```python\ndef findPerformersTagsbyID(self, id):\n    query = """query findPerformer($id: ID!){\n  findPerformer(id: $id){\n    tags{\n      name\n    }\n  }\n  \n}"""\n    variables = {\'id\': id}\n    result = self.__callGraphQL(query, variables)\n    performers_tags = result["findPerformer"]["tags"]\n    performers_tags_names = [performers_tag["name"] for performers_tag in performers_tags]\n    return performers_tags_names\n```\nNote that this function returns a list of tags for a performer, rather than a dictionary with a single key `\'tags\'`. If you want to check whether a performer has more than one tag, you can modify the `upscale_PerformerImage` function to check the length of the list returned by `findPerformersTagsbyID`:\n```python\ndef upscale_PerformerImage(self):\n    # ...\n    tags = self.findPerformersTagsbyID(performer_id)\n    if len(tags) > 1:\n        self.error(f"Performer with ID \'{performer_id}\' is tagged with more than one value")\n        continue\n    # ...\n```', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1057, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Based on the script you provided, you could modify the `get_square_footage` function to parse the response and extract the square footage from the space name variable as follows:\n```python\ndef get_square_footage(space_id):\n    space = hc.spaces.get(space_id)\n    space_name = space.get(\'name\')\n    # Extract square footage from the space name\n    square_footage = re.search(r\'\\b(\\d+)\\s*sq(ft|\\.? feet)\\b\', space_name, re.IGNORECASE)\n    if square_footage:\n        square_footage = int(square_footage.group(1))\n    else:\n        square_footage = None\n    return square_footage\n```\nIn this modified function, we use the `re.search` function to search for a pattern that matches a number followed by "sqft", "sq. ft", or "square feet" in the space name, and extract the number. The `\\b` characters match word boundaries, and `re.IGNORECASE` makes the pattern case-insensitive. If a match is found, we convert the matched number to an integer and return it. If no match is found, we return `None`.\n\nThis function assumes that the square footage is always specified in the space name in one of the specified formats. If this is not the case, you may need to modify the regular expression pattern to match the format used in your space names.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1105, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Students mix drops of differently colored food dye in a small bowl of milk to create new colors, allowing them to explore the names of the colors in Russian.\n\nMaterials\nRed, blue, and yellow food dye\nSmall bowls\nMilk (enough to fill each small bowl)\nToothpicks or small spoons for stirring\n\nProcedure\nFirst Session\nPour a small amount of milk into each bowl. Show students three bottles of food dye and ask, "Какого цвета это?" (Kakogo tsveta eto?, "What color is this?") Have students respond with the correct color. If they can\'t remember, say it for them and have them repeat it.\n\nDemonstrate how to add a drop of one color of food dye into a bowl of milk and mix it gently with a toothpick or small spoon.\n\nLet each student choose a color of their choice and add a drop to their bowl of milk, stirring gently. As they do this, ask them, "Какого цвета это?"\n\nTeach students two additional phrases:\n\n* "Это светлое" (Eto svetloe, "It\'s light")\n* "Это темное" (Eto temnoe, "It\'s dark")\n\nEncourage students to use these phrases to describe the shade of the color in their bowls of milk.\n\nLet students play independently with their bowls of colored milk. As they do, go around and ask them, "Какого цвета это?"\n\nSecond Session\nTell students you are going to perform a magic trick by mixing two colors of food dye in a bowl of milk to create a new color. Demonstrate by adding drops of two different colors and stirring gently.\n\nHave students try it with their own bowls of colored milk. As they do this, ask them, "Теперь какого цвета это?" (Teper\' kakogo tsveta eto?, "Now, what color is it?")\n\nAllow students to experiment with other combinations and observe the different color effects created by mixing the food dyes in milk. Encourage them to use the additional phrases learned to describe the shade and color of their creations.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1035, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Based on the information provided in the first content, the following are potential headings for an article about Deed of Novation:\n\n1. What is a Deed of Novation?\n2. How is a Deed of Novation different from a Contract?\n3. Why Engage a Commercial Contract Lawyer to Review a Deed of Novation?\n4. The Purpose of a Deed of Novation\n5. Options when Deciding How to Address a Novation\n6. Risks Associated with a Deed of Novation\n7. How to Draft a Deed of Novation\n8. How a Deed of Novation Needs to be Signed\n\nThese headings could be adjusted or combined as necessary to create a well-structured and informative article.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1050, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'What specific question do you have regarding the business RFP?', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1172, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Certainly, there are other common disputes related to distribution agreements, including:\n\n1. Delayed delivery of goods: The distributor may claim that the manufacturer or supplier failed to deliver goods on time, impacting business operations or causing financial losses.\nEvidence required: The contract specifying delivery terms, records of delivery dates and delays, correspondence related to delivery disputes, and documentation of any financial impact resulting from delayed deliveries.\n2. Transit damage: The distributor may claim that the goods were damaged during transportation and are now unsellable or require costly repairs.\nEvidence required: The contract specifying responsibility for transportation and handling, photographs or records of damaged goods, inspection reports, correspondence related to transit damage disputes, and documentation of any financial impact due to damaged goods.\n3. Quantity disputes: One party may claim that the goods delivered were in a lesser quantity than agreed upon in the contract.\nEvidence required: The contract specifying quantity terms, records of orders and deliveries, correspondence related to quantity disputes, and documentation of any financial impact due to the disputed quantities.\n4. Warranty and return disputes: The parties may disagree on warranty claims, return policies, or the handling of returned goods.\nEvidence required: The contract specifying warranty and return terms, records of warranty claims and returns, correspondence related to warranty and return disputes, and documentation of any financial impact due to warranty or return disagreements.\n5. Price disputes: The parties may disagree on the pricing of goods or changes to pricing over time.\nEvidence required: The contract specifying pricing terms, records of agreed-upon prices and any price changes, correspondence related to pricing disputes, and documentation of any financial impact due to pricing disagreements.\n6. Unfair competition: One party may claim that the other party engaged in unfair competition, such as price undercutting or predatory practices.\nEvidence required: The contract specifying any competition-related terms, proof of the alleged unfair competition practices, correspondence related to competition disputes, and documentation of any financial impact due to the alleged unfair competition.\n7. Regulatory compliance: One party may claim that the other party failed to comply with applicable laws and regulations, resulting in penalties or other negative consequences.\nEvidence required: The contract specifying compliance responsibilities, records of regulatory violations, correspondence related to compliance disputes, and documentation of any penalties or financial impact due to non-compliance.\n\nThis list is not exhaustive, as various other disputes can arise depending on the specific terms of the distribution agreement and the circumstances surrounding the business relationship. However, this expanded list should provide a better understanding of the common disputes that can occur in distribution agreements.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1407, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': "Sure, here's the updated code:\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nimport re\nimport nltk\nnltk.download('omw-1.4')\nnltk.download('wordnet')\nfrom nltk.corpus import stopwords\nfrom nltk.tokenize import word_tokenize\nfrom nltk.stem import WordNetLemmatizer\nfrom sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import KFold\nfrom sklearn.utils.class_weight import compute_class_weight\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\nfrom sklearn.metrics import f1_score\nfrom auto_tqdm import tqdm\n\n# Set seed value\nnp.random.seed(42)\n\n# Load data\ntrain_df = pd.read_csv('train.csv')\ntest_df = pd.read_csv('test.csv')\n\n# Compute class weights\nclass_weights = compute_class_weight(class_weight='balanced', classes=np.unique(train_df['label']), y=train_df['label'])\n\n# Define text pre-processing functions\ndef preprocess_text(text):\n    # Remove non-alphabetic characters\n    text = re.sub(r'[^a-zA-Z\\s]', '', text)\n    # Convert to lowercase\n    text = text.lower()\n    # Tokenize\n    tokens = word_tokenize(text)\n    # Remove stopwords\n    tokens = [token for token in tokens if token not in stopwords.words('english')]\n    # Lemmatize\n    lemmatizer = WordNetLemmatizer()\n    tokens = [lemmatizer.lemmatize(token) for token in tokens]\n    # Join tokens back into a string\n    text = ' '.join(tokens)\n    return text\n\n# Preprocess train and test text data with progress bar\nwith tqdm(total=len(train_df), desc='Preprocessing train text', dynamic_ncols=True) as pbar:\n    train_df['text'] = train_df['text'].apply(lambda x: preprocess_text(x))\n    pbar.update(len(train_df))\n\nwith tqdm(total=len(test_df), desc='Preprocessing test text', dynamic_ncols=True) as pbar:\n    test_df['text'] = test_df['text'].apply(lambda x: preprocess_text(x))\n    pbar.update(len(test_df))\n\n# Define KFold cross-validation with progress bar\nkf = KFold(n_splits=5, shuffle=True, random_state=42)\ntotal_iterations = kf.get_n_splits(train_df) * 2  # 2 iterations per fold (train\n```", 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1091, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
