{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'In natural language processing, phrasal level constructs are units of language that are composed of more than one word. Some common examples of phrasal level constructs include:\n\n* Phrases: A phrase is a group of words that function as a single unit in a sentence. Examples include noun phrases (e.g. "the big red apple"), verb phrases (e.g. "is running"), and prepositional phrases (e.g. "in the park").\n* Clauses: A clause is a group of words that contains a subject and a predicate. There are two types of clauses: independent clauses (e.g. "I am going to the store") and dependent clauses (e.g. "when I finish my work").\n* Sentences: A sentence is a group of words that express a complete thought. Sentences can be simple (e.g. "I am happy"), compound (e.g. "I am happy, but I am also tired"), or complex (e.g. "I am happy, even though I am also tired").\n* Paragraphs: A paragraph is a group of sentences that discuss a single topic or idea.\n* Discourse: It is a unit of language that goes beyond the sentence level and encompasses multiple sentences or even text segments. It takes into account the relationships between sentences and their roles in shaping the meaning of a text.\n\nIt\'s', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1116, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': "It looks like the issue is that you are adding the `ContentNavigationDrawer` widget twice in your kv file. The first time it's added as a child of the `MainScreen` rule and the second time it's added as a child of the `MDNavigationDrawer` widget. This will cause the `ContentNavigationDrawer` to appear twice on the screen. To fix this, you should remove the `ContentNavigationDrawer` widget from the `MainScreen` rule, and keep the one that is added as a child of the `MDNavigationDrawer` widget.\n\nAlso, you are using `ContentNavigationDrawer` widget inside MDNavigationDrawer. It is not required you can directly add the items inside the `MDNavigationDrawer`.", 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1051, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'This is an example of a personal statement where the author shares their passion for neuroscience and engineering, sparked by their fascination with The Matrix movie as a child. They describe their academic journey and relevant research experiences, including participating in a summer undergraduate research program in Computational Neuroscience at Carnegie Mellon University and the University of Pittsburgh. They conclude by discussing their current research interests in brain-computer interface decoders.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1857, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'The application proposes research into nanotransducers as functional neural interfaces for bidirectional communication with the brain. The proposed study aims to find optimal nanotransducers for imaging neurons, stimulating neurons, and relaying signals. The study will investigate different imaging methods, such as quantum dot imaging, voltage-sensitive dye imaging, and genetically encoded voltage indicators. For stimulation, the study will compare magnetoelectric, piezoelectric, and optoelectric nanotransducers with conventional electrode-based stimulation. The research will be conducted using Caenorhabditis elegans as the model system, investigating the placement of the nanotransducers and their potential toxicity.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1209, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': "Drongo's application describes their research focus on nano functional neural interfaces (nFNIs) to allow for high-resolution, bidirectional communication with the nervous system while being minimally invasive. They have research experience in neuroscience, including an internship in computational neuroscience where they worked in Dr. Aaron Batista's lab and investigated the impact of neuron dropping on decoder performance. Drongo is also currently part of a team investigating the dosing of deep brain stimulation (DBS) in disorders such as Parkinson’s Disease and is conducting an independent study into neural nanotransducers to develop their dissertation question. Despite facing obstacles, Drongo is excelling in their coursework and research and hopes to lead a fulfilling research career at UT Austin.", 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1123, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': "To perform black box testing on the SecureHelp web application with a focus on identifying security vulnerabilities, follow these steps:\n\n1. Test Authentication and Session Management:\na. Attempt to brute-force login credentials.\nb. Test for weak or default passwords.\nc. Test session fixation vulnerabilities.\nd. Test for proper session timeouts and secure logout functionality.\ne. Test for password reset and recovery vulnerabilities.\n2. Test Access Controls and Authorization:\na. Test for Insecure Direct Object References (IDOR) by manipulating URLs or request parameters.\nb. Test for horizontal and vertical privilege escalation.\nc. Test for unauthorized access to sensitive data or functionality.\n3. Test Input Validation and Injection Flaws:\na. Test for SQL injection, command injection, and other injection vulnerabilities.\nb. Test for Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities.\nc. Test for XML External Entity (XXE) vulnerabilities, if applicable.\n4. Test for Security Misconfigurations:\na. Check for overly permissive CORS policies.\nb. Test for missing security headers (e.g., Content-Security-Policy, X-Content-Type-Options).\nc. Check for unnecessary enabled services or exposed ports.\nd. Test for weak TLS/SSL configurations and certificates.\n5. Test for Sensitive Data Exposure:\na. Test for sensitive data being transmitted in clear text.\nb. Check for sensitive data leakage through logs, error messages, or URLs.\nc. Test for proper encryption of sensitive data at rest.\n6. Test for Broken Authentication:\na. Test for enumeration of valid usernames.\nb. Test for user impersonation.\n7. Test for Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) vulnerabilities:\na. Check for CSRF tokens and proper validation.\nb. Test for state-changing requests without CSRF tokens.\n8. Test for Clickjacking vulnerabilities:\na. Check for proper implementation of X-Frame-Options header or Content Security Policy 'frame-ancestors' directive.\n9. Test for Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) vulnerabilities:\na. Test the application for SSRF vulnerabilities by manipulating URLs or request parameters.\n10. Test for proper logging and monitoring:\na. Ensure that security events are logged and monitored.\n\nUse various tools and libraries to perform these tests, such as:\n\n* OWASP ZAP or Burp Suite for automated vulnerability scanning.\n* Postman or curl for manual API testing.\n* Browser developer tools for testing front-end and analyzing network requests.\n* SQLMap for SQL injection testing.\n\nRemember to document your test cases, test data, and results, so you can track the application's security posture, identify any vulnerabilities, and ensure they are addressed by the development team.", 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1095, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': "I see the issue now. The problem is that `MessagePassing` in PyTorch Geometric assumes that the node features have a shape of `(num_nodes, num_features)`, and it doesn't support higher-dimensional features out-of-the-box.\n\nHowever, we can work around this limitation by flattening and reshaping the node features before and after the message-passing process. Here's an updated version of your code that should work without throwing any errors:\n```python\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport torch_geometric.nn as pyg_nn\nimport torch_geometric.data as pyg_data\nfrom torch_geometric.nn import MessagePassing\n\nclass EdgeConv2DGCNConv(MessagePassing):\n    def __init__(self, in_channels, out_channels):\n        super(EdgeConv2DGCNConv, self).__init__(aggr='add')\n        self.lin = nn.Conv2d(in_channels * 2, out_channels, 3, 1)\n\n    def forward(self, x, edge_index, edge_attr):\n        x_flat = x.view(x.size(0), -1)  # Flatten x before propagation\n        out = self.propagate(edge_index, x=x_flat, edge_attr=edge_attr)\n        return out.view(x.size(0), self.lin.out_channels, x.size(2), x.size(3))  # Reshape output to the original shape\n\n    def message(self, x_j, edge_attr):\n        x_j = x_j.view(-1, x_j.size(1) // 9, 3, 3)  # Reshape x_j\n        edge_attr = edge_attr.view(-1, edge_attr.size(1) // 9, 3, 3)  # Reshape edge_attr\n        edge_attr = torch.cat([x_j, edge_attr], dim=1)\n        return self.lin(edge_attr).view(edge_attr.size(0), -1)  # Flatten output\n\nclass GNNClassifier(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, in_channels, hidden_channels, num_classes):\n        super(GNNClassifier, self).__init__()\n        self.gnn = EdgeConv2DGCNConv(in_channels, hidden_channels)\n        self.pool = pyg_nn.global_mean_pool\n        self.classifier = nn.Linear(hidden_channels * 9, num_classes)\n\n    def forward(self, data):\n        x, edge_index, edge_attr = data.x, data.edge_index, data.edge_attr\n        x = self.gnn(x, edge_index, edge_attr)\n        x = torch.relu(x)\n        x_flat = x.view(x.size(0), -1)  # Flatten x before pooling\n        x = self.pool(x_flat, data.batch)\n        out = self.classifier(x)\n        return out\n\nnum_nodes = 100\nin_channels = 16\nhidden_channels = 32\nnum_classes = 5\nbatch_size = 2\n\nnode_features = torch.rand((num_nodes, in_channels, 3, 3))\nedge_features = torch.rand((num_nodes, in_channels, 3, 3))\nedge_index = torch.randint(0, num_nodes, (2, num_nodes))\nbatch = torch.tensor([0] * (num_nodes // 2) + [1] * (num_nodes // 2))\n\ndata = pyg_data.Data(x=node_features, edge_index=edge_index, edge_attr=edge_features, batch=batch)\n\nmodel = GNNClassifier(in_channels, hidden_channels, num_classes)\noutput = model(data)\nprint(output)\n```\nNow, the `EdgeConv2DGCNConv` class flattens and reshapes the node features before and after the message-passing process, making it compatible with", 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1048, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'There are a few potential security vulnerabilities and code smells in the provided code:\n\n1. Insecure deserialization: In the VerificationView, the method `urlsafe_base64_decode` is used to deserialize the user ID from the URL. This can be potentially exploited if an attacker can create a crafted URL with a malicious payload.\n2. Lack of exception specificity: In some instances, such as in VerificationView, ResetPasswordView, and SetNewPasswordView, a generic `except Exception` is used to handle exceptions. This can lead to unintended behavior, as the code will suppress all types of exceptions without providing proper error handling. It\'s better to catch specific exceptions to avoid unexpected issues.\n3. Information disclosure: The response message in PasswordResetEmailView gives an indication of whether a user with the given email and username exists in the system or not. An attacker can use this information to enumerate valid user accounts. A more generic message, such as "If the provided email and username are valid, you will shortly receive a link to reset your password", would be more secure.\n4. Insecure object references: In DocumentDownloadView, the user\'s permission to download a document is checked using an if-else statement, which could be improved by using a more robust permission handling mechanism. This would help prevent unauthorized access to documents.\n5. Code smells: Some imports are duplicated, such as `from django.contrib.sites.shortcuts import get_current_site`. Removing duplicate imports would improve code readability and maintainability.\n\nWhile the code may work as intended, addressing these potential security vulnerabilities and code smells would make it more secure and robust.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1365, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
