{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Here is the final report:\n\nDesired Skills, Knowledge, and Qualifications for an Instructional Designer:\n\n1. Instructional design models & principles (e.g. ADDIE, Dick and Carey) - MATCH: "using principles and practices of instructional design"\n2. adult learning theory - MATCH: "using principles and practices of instructional design and adult learning theory."\n3. Organizational development - NOT MATCH\n4. Accessing and analyzing DATA - NOT MATCH\n5. Theories of leadership - NOT MATCH\n6. Gamification - NOT MATCH\n7. Blended learning techniques - NOT MATCH\n8. Formative and summative evaluation - NOT MATCH\n9. AR/VR Environments - NOT MATCH\n10. Agile methodology (e.g. Scrum) - NOT MATCH\n11. Interface design - NOT MATCH\n12. Classroom-based technology integration techniques - NOT MATCH\n13. Human resources management - NOT MATCH\n14. Professional development - NOT MATCH\n15. Customer service - NOT MATCH\n16. Motivation theories (e.g. ARCS) - NOT MATCH\n17. Markup languages (e.g. HTML/XHTML/XML) - NOT MATCH\n18. Assessment methods (e.g. criterion-referenced) - NOT MATCH\n19. Online/blended programme management - NOT MATCH\n20. Instructional simulation and game design - NOT MATCH\n21. 3D modelling tools (e.g. Maya) - NOT MATCH\n22. SWOT analysis - NOT MATCH\n23. Project management software (e.g. Microsoft Project) - NOT MATCH\n24. Learning object standards (e.g. SCORM) - NOT MATCH\n25. Laws, policies and procedures in training programmes - NOT MATCH\n26. Cloud technologies - NOT MATCH\n27. Business intelligence (e.g. SAP BW) - NOT MATCH\n28. Programming languages (e.g. C++) - NOT MATCH\n29. Cascading Style Sheets (CSS) - NOT MATCH\n30. Social media technologies (e.g. Twitter) - NOT MATCH\n31. Web design principles - NOT MATCH\n32. Assessment software (e.g. Respondus) - NOT MATCH\n33. Audio software (e.g. Audacity) - NOT MATCH\n34. Streaming video technology (e.g. Windows Media Server) - NOT MATCH\n35. Project management principles (e.g. PMBOK) - NOT MATCH\n36. Industry Standard - NOT MATCH\n37. Virtual classrooms (e.g. Wimba or Elluminate! Live) - NOT MATCH\n38. Virtual environments (e.g. SecondLife) - NOT MATCH\n39. Cognitive learning theory (e.g. Cognitive Load Theory) - NOT MATCH\n40. Constructivism - NOT MATCH\n41. Mobile learning platforms (e.g. Android) - NOT MATCH\n42. Synchronous distance learning methodologies (e.g. Blackboard Collaborate) - NOT MATCH\n43. Human Performance Technology principles - NOT MATCH\n44. Flipped classroom (blended) - NOT MATCH\n45. Mobile application development - NOT MATCH\n46. Desktop publishing software (e.g. PageMaker) - NOT MATCH\n47. Web authoring tools (e.g. Dreamweaver) - NOT MATCH\n48. Operating system software (e.g. Windows 7) - NOT MATCH\n49. Game engines (e', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1160, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': "This code simulates the operation of the Go-Back-N protocol for reliable data transfer over a noisy channel. It simulates two entities, A and B, communicating over a network.\n\nThe main function initializes the simulation, initializes entities A and B, and then repeatedly pulls events off the event list and simulates them until the event list is empty.\n\nThe struct `event` represents an event in the simulation, with the following fields:\n\n* `evtime`: the time at which the event occurs\n* `evtype`: the type of the event (0 = TIMER\\_INTERRUPT, 1 = FROM\\_LAYER5, 2 = FROM\\_LAYER3)\n* `eventity`: the entity where the event occurs (0 = A, 1 = B)\n* `pktptr`: a pointer to the packet associated with the event (if any)\n* `prev`: a pointer to the previous event in the event list\n* `next`: a pointer to the next event in the event list\n\nThe `evlist` variable is a pointer to the first event in the event list.\n\nThe `insertevent` function inserts an event into the event list in order of increasing `evtime`.\n\nThe `init` function initializes the simulator and prompts the user to enter simulation parameters such as the number of messages to simulate, the time between messages from sender's layer 5, and the channel pattern string. It also initializes the window size and allocates memory for the window buffer.\n\nThe `A_init` and `B_init` functions initialize entities A and B, respectively.\n\nThe `generate_next_arrival` function generates the next arrival event and inserts it into the event list. The arrival rate of messages from layer 5 is specified by the `lambda` parameter.\n\nThe `A_output` and `B_output` functions are called by entities A and B, respectively, to send packets to the other entity. They construct packets, compute their checksums, and add them to the window buffer if there is space. If the packet being sent is the first packet in the window, they start the timer.\n\nThe `A_input` and `B_input` functions are called by entities A and B, respectively, to handle incoming packets. They check the packet's checksum and process it according to the protocol. If the packet contains new data, they pass it up to layer 5.\n\nThe `A_timerinterrupt` function is called by entity A when the timer for the oldest unacknowledged packet in the window expires. It resends all unacknowledged packets in the window and restarts the timer.\n\nThe `print_info` function prints out statistics about the simulation, such as the number of packets sent, lost, and corrupted.", 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1222, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'It looks like you are trying to use a type named `TextFile` as a document in your app, but it is not conforming to the `FileDocument` protocol.\n\nIn order to use a type as a document in your app, it must conform to the `FileDocument` protocol. This protocol defines the methods and properties that a type must implement in order to be used as a document in a file-based app.\n\nTo fix the error, you need to make sure that your `TextFile` type conforms to the `FileDocument` protocol. Here is an example of how you might do this:\n```swift\nstruct TextFile: FileDocument {\n    var fileURL: URL\n    var content: String\n\n    init(fileURL: URL, content: String) {\n        self.fileURL = fileURL\n        self.content = content\n    }\n\n    static var readableContentTypes: [String] {\n        // Return an array of the file types that your app can read\n        // For example:\n        return ["public.text"]\n    }\n\n    static func open(fileURL: URL, contentType: String) throws -> TextFile {\n        // Read the contents of the file at the given URL and return a new TextFile instance\n        let content = try String(contentsOf: fileURL)\n        return TextFile(fileURL: fileURL, content: content)\n    }\n\n    func save(to fileURL: URL, for contentType: String) throws {\n        // Write the contents of the\n```', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1118, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Bulleted version:\n\n* Wang Zheng\'s book "Never Forget National Humiliation" argues that understanding a country requires reading its textbooks\n* History textbooks play a crucial role in shaping national consciousness, often involving "othering" of those who do not belong\n* Wang\'s book does not discuss "memories" of Tibet, Xinjiang, or Mongolia/Inner Mongolia, despite these regions comprising half of China\'s territory\n* Many scholars equate "Chineseness" with the culture and history of the Han majority and "Han" ancestry\n* A study of junior secondary textbooks published by the People\'s Education Press (PEP) in 2001-2002 highlighted a state-centric perspective, monolithic vision of Chinese nationhood, portrayal of "minority" histories in relation to "China," teleology of unification based on the "One China Principle," and portrayal of challenges to Chinese sovereignty as the result of foreign imperialism\n* A study of senior high school textbooks published in 1951, 1956, and 2003 showed a shift from a Han-exclusivist vision of Chinese history to a more inclusive, multi-ethnic one by 2003, attributed to efforts of PEP editors in the late 1970s and early 1980s and the consolidation of Chinese control over "minority" regions\n* A study of junior secondary history textbooks published by the PEP in 2006 and 2013 found that while there were some positive changes, the texts still emphasized a state-centric perspective and a monolithic vision of Chinese nationhood, and downplayed the agency of "minority" groups\n* The portrayal of Tibet, Xinjiang, and Inner Mongolia in these texts changed over time, with increasing recognition of their distinct histories and cultures, but their integration into the Chinese nation was presented as inevitable and natural\n* The portrayal of Taiwan and Hong Kong changed little over time, with a focus on their eventual reunification with China\n* The portrayal of foreign imperialism in the texts changed over time, with a shift from a negative portrayal of Western imperialism to a negative portrayal of Japanese imperialism\n* The texts were produced through a complex process involving the PEP, the State Council\'s General Office, the State Ethnic Affairs Commission, and the National Ethnic Affairs Committee, with the PEP and the State Council\'s General Office having the most influence\n* The texts reflect the interests and ideologies of the individuals and groups involved in their production and the broader political context in which they were produced', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1126, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Bulleted version:\n\n* Recent research has found generally unfavourable portrayal and marginal status of ethnic minorities in Chinese school textbooks for various subjects\n* Han chauvinism and incomplete, stereotypical information about minorities is prevalent in primary textbooks for "Chinese Language," "Moral Education and Life," and "Moral Education and Society"\n* Lack of systematic discussion of minority cultures in junior secondary history textbooks\n* Textbooks for minority languages such as Korean and Uyghur reflect assumptions of superiority and centrality of Han culture\n* Research on Chinese textbooks often uses "critical discourse theory," but this may not be as useful in the case of China due to the cruder politics of nation-building directed by a Han-dominated authoritarian state\n* Representation of minority ethnic groups in Chinese textbooks must be seen in the context of Chinese nationalism and the tensions between pseudo-biological conceptions of nationhood and official attempts to promote a multicultural vision of the "Chinese nation"\n* Shifts in CCP ideology in Beijing are central to analysis of representation of minority ethnic groups in Chinese history textbooks\n* Textbook portrayals of minorities have changed over time, with a shift from negative portrayal of Western imperialism to negative portrayal of Japanese imperialism\n* Textbook portrayal of Tibet, Xinjiang, and Inner Mongolia has changed over time, with increasing recognition of their distinct histories and cultures, but their integration into the Chinese nation is presented as inevitable and natural\n* Textbook portrayal of Taiwan and Hong Kong has changed little over time, with a focus on their eventual reunification with China\n* The portrayals of minorities in the texts reflect the interests and ideologies of the individuals and groups involved in their production and the broader political context in which they were produced\n* The texts also reflect the CCP\'s efforts to assert its sovereignty over frontier regions and its desire to promote national unity and social stability', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1126, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Text:\n\nIntroduction\nWang Zheng, in his book Never Forget National Humiliation: Historical Memory in Chinese Politics and Foreign Relations (2012), writes portentously that “to understand a country, … one must read its textbooks” (7). While perhaps revelatory for specialists in international relations, this insight will seem somewhat banal to those who research the history and politics of education. Whether in centralized, bureaucratized systems for determining “official knowledge” (as across contemporary East Asia), or in contexts where power is ostensibly more dispersed (e.g., America), the crucial role assigned to history textbooks in moulding national consciousness has been noted by numerous scholars (Foster & Crawford, 2006; Hein & Selden, 2000; Vickers & Jones, 2005). And this typically involves explicit or implicit “othering” of those who do not belong, reflecting the interests and prejudices of those who control textbook production, vetting and selection.\n\nBulleted version:\n\n* Wang Zheng argues that understanding a country requires reading its textbooks\n* The role of history textbooks in shaping national consciousness is well-known in the field of education research\n* Textbooks often involve "othering" of those who do not belong and reflect the interests and prejudices of those who control production, vetting, and selection.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1126, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': '* 1992 PEP texts aim to construct a multi-ethnic vision of China\'s past\n* Political climate in the late 1980s and early 1990s was more liberal compared to the post-Tiananmen crackdown period\n* Texts emphasize a multi-ethnic concept of China and highlight the contributions of non-Han groups to the country\'s development\n* 1992 texts show a more positive portrayal of the Mongol Yuan and Manchu Qing dynasties, highlighting their economic and social achievements and their governance strategies that respected the cultural traditions of their subjects\n* Texts also challenge traditional Han-centric views of inter-ethnic conflict, presenting a more balanced view of the actions of both Han and non-Han groups\n* The 1992 texts go further in justifying "minority" attacks on Han-dominated states, casting the Han as aggressors and portraying the Manchu Qing\'s victory over the Ming as a "famous battle in Chinese military history"\n* Political changes in the 1990s lead to revisions in curriculum and textbook content, with a focus on strengthening patriotism and the CCP\'s leading role\n* 1992 edition of the texts features "touching stories of the patriotic deeds of ethnic minorities" and further emphasizes the multi-ethnic nature of China\'s past\n* Texts also feature more detailed coverage of the experiences of ethnic minorities under CCP rule, including their participation in the revolution and contributions to socialist construction\n* However, the focus on patriotism and the CCP\'s leadership role leads to a downplaying of the negative impacts of certain policies on ethnic minorities, such as the Cultural Revolution and the "Strike Hard" campaign.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1505, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': '* The 2001-2 PEP history textbooks placed greater emphasis on Chinese national unity, rather than diversity and inter-ethnic "equality"\n* This shift in focus reflected political and cultural currents in the 1990s and the personal predilections of the new chief editors, Qi Jiaxiang and Ma Zhibing\n* However, ethnic pluralism remained an important theme in the 2001-2 textbooks, with new sections on inter-ethnic relations during the Tang and Qing Dynasties added\n* Material on the origins and customs of "minorities" was reinterpreted or removed in the 2001-2 textbooks, with the term "tribe" or "ethnicity" replaced with "kingdom" and many tribal names removed from maps\n* The 2001-2 textbooks also downplayed the role of non-Han ethnic groups in Chinese history and emphasized the "civilizing" role of the Han majority\n* This shift towards a more ethnocultural conception of the Chinese nation reflected the CCP\'s efforts to bolster national unity in the face of increasing economic and social challenges', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1362, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'The People\'s Education Press (PEP) history textbooks in China have undergone significant changes over the years, reflecting the shifting political and ideological climate in the country. In the 1990s, patriotism was given a prominent role in the curriculum in response to the 1989 student movement and the push for market-oriented economic reform. This led to revisions in the curricula and textbooks, with a focus on modern and contemporary Chinese history and the promotion of patriotism. The 2001-2 edition of the PEP history textbooks, edited by Qi Jiaxiang and Ma Zhibing, placed a greater emphasis on ethnonationalism and the Yellow Emperor, a semi-mythical figure traditionally seen as the ancestor of the Han people. This shift reflected the political and cultural currents of the 1990s, but may also have been influenced by the personal views of the new chief editors. Despite this emphasis on unity, the 2001-2 edition also included some recognition of diversity and inter-ethnic relations, including a new section on inter-ethnic relations during the Qing Dynasty and activities encouraging students to learn about and respect "minority" customs and culture. However, the representation of non-Han ethnic groups remained limited and heavily shaped by the dominant narrative of national unity. In the 2010 edition, edited by Wang Niansun, there was a return to a more diverse and multi-ethnic perspective, with a greater focus on the contributions and cultures of non-Han ethnic groups. This shift may have been influenced by the influence of global discourses on multiculturalism and the increasing recognition of China\'s diversity within the country. However, the 2010 edition also faced criticism for downplaying the role of the CCP and emphasizing', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1371, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'In summary, herbal medicine faces various challenges in being integrated into modern medical practices due to quality, safety,', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1837, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
