{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Summary in outline format:\n\nSEC. 11. PENALTIES.\n(a) Unlawful Acts.\n(1) It is unlawful to violate, attempt to violate, conspire to violate, or cause a violation of any regulation, order, mitigation measure, regulation, or prohibition issued under this Act.\n(2) Specific unlawful acts.\n(A) Engaging in prohibited conduct.\n(B) Aiding, abetting, counselling, commanding, inducing, procuring, permitting, or approving prohibited acts.\n(C) Soliciting or attempting a violation.\n(D) Conspiring or acting in concert with others to commit a violation.\n(E) Making false or misleading statements, representations, or certifications.\n(F) Engaging in transactions with intent to evade provisions.\n(G) Failing or refusing to comply with reporting or recordkeeping requirements.\n(H) Altering any order, direction, mitigation measure, or authorization issued under this Act or any related regulation.\n(3) Additional requirements.\n(A) Continuation of effect.\n(B) Notification.\n(b) Civil Penalties.\n(1) Fine of up to $250,000 or twice the value of the transaction.\n(2) Revocation of any mitigation measure or authorization issued under this Act to the person.\n(c) Criminal Penalties.\n(1) Fine of up to $1,000,000 or imprisonment for up to 20 years, or both.\n(2) Civil forfeiture.\n(A) Forfeiture of property used or intended to be used, in any manner, to commit or facilitate a violation or attempted violation.\n(B) Procedure.\n(3) Criminal forfeiture.\n(A) Forfeiture of property used or intended to be used, in any manner, to commit or facilitate a violation or attempted violation.\n(B) Procedure.\n\nOverall summary:\n\nSection 11 outlines the penalties for violating any regulation, order, mitigation measure, regulation, or prohibition issued under this Act. It is unlawful to violate, attempt to violate, conspire to violate, or cause a violation of any such directive. Specific unlawful acts are listed, including making false or misleading statements and engaging in transactions with the intent to evade provisions. Civil penalties may include a fine or revocation of authorization. Criminal penalties may include fines, imprisonment, and forfeiture of property used or intended to be used to commit a violation. The procedures for civil and criminal forfeiture are also outlined.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1207, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
