{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Sure, here\'s a new description for the video script we created for idea 1 "Why Eating More Can Actually Help You Lose Weight":\n\nAre you tired of feeling hungry all the time in your weight loss journey? What if we told you that you could eat more and still lose weight? That\'s right, folks! In this video, we\'re going to explore the surprising science behind how eating more can actually help you shed those extra pounds. We\'ll bust some common weight loss myths and provide tips for incorporating metabolism-boosting foods into your diet. Plus, stick around until the end for a bonus tip that can take your weight loss efforts to the next level. Don\'t miss out on this game-changing information - hit that play button now and join us for the ultimate guide to healthy weight loss!', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1417, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'The lack of reliable and flexible accountability systems is one of the major challenges that NGOs in Africa face when providing support to vulnerable communities. This can make it difficult for NGOs to track and monitor the use of their resources, and can create opportunities for mismanagement and corruption. Additionally, the lack of accountability can make it difficult for NGOs to provide accurate and detailed reports to donors and other funders, which can impact their ability to secure funding and support for their programs.\n\nAnother challenge that NGOs in Africa often face is the safety of beneficiaries. Vulnerable communities may be located in remote or insecure areas, and providing support to these communities can put NGO staff and beneficiaries at risk. This can make it difficult for NGOs to extend support to these communities, and can limit their ability to provide the assistance that is needed.\n\nThe lack of internet access, or weak internet connection, is also a major challenge for NGOs in Africa. Many remote and vulnerable communities in Africa have limited or no access to the internet, which can make it difficult for NGOs to provide support and assistance. This can limit the ability of NGOs to communicate with beneficiaries and other stakeholders, and can make it difficult to monitor and evaluate the impact of their programs.\n\nDifficulties in accessibility and reaching in-need communities can also be a challenge for NGOs in Africa. Vulnerable communities may be located in remote or difficult-to-reach areas, which can make it difficult for NGOs to provide support. Additionally, accessibility', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1399, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'This script is a Python Tkinter implementation of an advanced image zoom feature, similar to the one found in Google Maps. The script uses the Tkinter library for creating a GUI and the PIL library for opening and manipulating the image. The script creates a scrollable window that displays an image, and allows the user to zoom in and out of the image using the mouse wheel or by clicking and dragging the image. The script also uses a custom AutoScrollbar class that hides the scrollbar when it is not needed.\n\nThe script is using the Tkinter library to create a GUI, that has a canvas where it is displaying the image and two scrollbars (horizontal and vertical) that are only shown when needed. By using the mouse wheel you can zoom in and out of the image and also by clicking and dragging the image you can move it around the canvas.\n\nThis script is well written and organized, it uses classes and functions that makes it easy to read and understand, it also uses the PIL library to open the image, which is a powerful library for image processing, so this script can handle different image formats.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1158, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': "The Deeper Well section of the game and the Branch Coral character play an important role in expressing different aspects of Sunny's predicament and leading up to the next section of the game, Black Space.\n\nIn the Deeper Well section, the player delves deeper into Sunny's subconscious, where they encounter the Branch Coral character. The Branch Coral character represents a deeper level of Sunny's guilt and trauma, as it is surrounded by darkness and decay. The character is also tied to the theme of growth and change, as it appears to be growing out of Sunny's memories.\n\nAs the player progresses through the section, they discover that the Branch Coral is blocking their way to the next area, Black Space. This represents how Sunny's trauma and guilt are blocking his ability to confront and process his emotions, which is necessary for him to move forward.\n\nOnce the player reaches Black Space, they are confronted with the culmination of the Headspace section of the game. Black Space represents the darkest corners of Sunny's mind, where he is forced to confront his deepest fears and memories. The experience is both terrifying and cathartic, as Sunny is finally able to confront and process his emotions.\n\nOverall, the Deeper Well section and the Branch Coral character serve as a metaphor for the inner workings of Sunny's mind, and the obstacles he must overcome in order to come to terms with his past and move forward.", 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1684, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Here is the formatted article based on the script you provided:\n\nIntroduction to YouTube Shorts\n==============================\n\nYouTube Shorts is a new way to watch, discover, and create short content using nothing but your phone. In this article, we will walk you through what Shorts are, how to get started with them, and some best practices for using them.\n\nWhat are Shorts?\n----------------\n\nShorts are a new video experience that allows you to watch and create short, catchy videos right from your phone. They are vertical videos that are 60 seconds or less and provide an easy, lightweight way to create content. Plus, they connect you to YouTube\'s two billion users, giving them a new, customized viewing experience for endless laughs, learning, music discovery, and more.\n\nWhether you have been creating for years or are just getting started, you can instantly reach and entertain new audiences with our Shorts creation tools, which allow you to capture and edit videos on YouTube right from your phone. Alternatively, you can upload Shorts by following the standard upload flow on mobile, desktop, or your preferred device.\n\nIf you create Shorts using our Shorts creation tools, you can use our multi-segment camera to string multiple video clips together. You can add a song or sample audio from other Shorts or eligible YouTube videos. You can also add text after and control when it appears and disappears, control the speed of your video, and more.\n\nViewers can find Shorts by going into the dedicated Shorts tab. They can also discover them on the YouTube homepage, in their subscriptions, on your channel homepage with the Shorts "Featured" section, and more. Shorts will have their own viewing experience separate from regular videos, and once viewers click into a Short, they can scroll through to watch and enjoy more.\n\nWhy use Shorts?\n---------------\n\nPeople around the globe are watching more and more Shorts. The YouTube Shorts player has now surpassed 6.5 billion daily views globally. With its own separate viewing experience, think of Shorts as an additional way for you to create and reach new audiences who may have never seen your content before.\n\nShorts should not have any major impact on the performance of your regular YouTube videos. And we are keeping your subscribers in mind. We will only send Shorts notifications to subscribers who regularly watch Shorts content.\n\nHow to create a Short\n---------------------\n\nTo get started, tap the Create button, then tap Create a Short. If you want to add a song to record to, tap "Add music" to view a selection of tracks you can choose for free.\n\nTo record a clip, press and hold the Capture button or tap it to start recording, and then again to stop. You can tap Undo to remove the previous video clip you recorded or tap Redo to add it back. Tap Speed to change your recording speed, and tap Timer to choose the length of your countdown that shows before you start recording, and to choose where your recording stops.\n\nA line lets you match up the clip you are about to record with the previous clip. You can also add filters to your Short. Once you are done, tap Next to preview your Short. Here, you can add music, if you haven\'t already, or text to your completed Short.\n\nWhen you add music, you can adjust what part of the song you want to play and its volume. When you add text, you can use Timeline to add multiple text snippets and control when text appears and disappears.\n\nOnce you are happy with your Short, tap Next to add details like the title and visibility. We recommend adding #shorts in your title to help with discovery.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1214, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Yes, it is possible to create spheres instead of circles in a d3.js scatterplot. One way to do this is by using the `d3.js` `shape` functions such as `d3.symbol()` or `d3.symbolCircle()` to create the spheres. You can then use the `attr` method to set the `d` attribute of the SVG path element to the result of the `shape` function, and set the appropriate `transform` attribute to position and scale the spheres.\n\nFor example, you can create a sphere symbol using `d3.symbolCircle()` and set the size of the sphere by passing the size as the parameter of the `d3.symbolCircle()` function.\n```scss\nvar sphere = d3.symbol()\n    .type(d3.symbolCircle)\n    .size(50);\n```\nYou can then append the sphere to the scatterplot using the `attr` method to set the `d` attribute of the SVG path element to the result of the `shape` function.\n```kotlin\nsvg.selectAll("path")\n    .data(data)\n    .enter()\n    .append("path")\n    .attr("d", sphere)\n    .attr("transform", function(d) { \n        return "translate(" + x(d.x) + "," + y(d.y) + ")"; \n    });\n```\nYou can also use other symbol types such as d3.symbolDiamond, d3.symbolCross, d3.symbolSquare etc.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1075, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Based on the information provided in the first content, the following are potential headings for an article about Deed of Novation:\n\n1. What is a Deed of Novation?\n2. How is a Deed of Novation different from a Contract?\n3. Why Engage a Commercial Contract Lawyer to Review a Deed of Novation?\n4. The Purpose of a Deed of Novation\n5. Options when Deciding How to Address a Novation\n6. Risks Associated with a Deed of Novation\n7. How to Draft a Deed of Novation\n8. How a Deed of Novation Needs to be Signed\n\nThese headings could be adjusted or combined as necessary to create a well-structured and informative article.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1050, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Subject: Earth Day 2023 Involvement\n\nHi David,\n\nThank you for your response! Yes, we would love for you to be a part of Earth Day 2023. Although you are retired, your knowledge, experience, and passion for the environment can still contribute to the success of this historic event.\n\nThere are many ways to get involved, such as spreading the word, participating in local events, or even volunteering in any capacity that suits your interests and abilities. Every effort counts towards making a positive impact on our planet.\n\nTo discuss how you can contribute to Earth Day 2023, please feel free to book a time on my calendar using this link: <https://bit.ly/Meet-With-ForTreesClub>\n\nAlternatively, let me know your availability, and we can schedule a time that works best for you.\n\nLooking forward to discussing further and working together to make Earth Day 2023 a landmark event in our fight against climate change.\n\nBest regards,\n\nJo Peninsulas', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1102, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': "Jo Peninsulas 2:35 PM\nHi Mark Alan,\n\nIndeed, Watamu is a hidden gem with its beautiful natural landscapes and rich biodiversity. It's a perfect example of the kind of places we want to protect and preserve through our Earth Day 2023 initiatives.\n\nTo discuss Earth Day 2023 further and explore how you can be a part of it, you can use the following link to schedule a meeting at a time that works best for you: <https://bit.ly/Meet-With-ForTreesClub>\n\nWe're looking forward to discussing the event with you and finding ways to collaborate in making Earth Day 2023 a truly impactful and memorable experience.\n\nBest regards,\nJo", 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1257, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'This code defines two classes for loading and processing text data in a PyTorch dataset. The first class, `TextDataset`, loads text data from a given directory and splits it into training and testing sets. It also preprocesses the text data by tokenizing the captions and building a dictionary of word-to-index and index-to-word mappings. It also loads image data corresponding to each caption in the dataset.\n\nThe second class, `TextBertDataset`, is a subclass of `TextDataset` that preprocesses the captions using the `arabert_prep` preprocessing module and tokenizes the text using the pre-trained BERT tokenizer. This allows the model to work with a pre-trained BERT model and take advantage of its language modeling capabilities.\n\nIt is designed to handle Arabic text, as evidenced by the use of the `ArabertPreprocessor` module to preprocess the text, and the use of a pre-trained Arabic BERT model to tokenize the text.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1211, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': "To fix this issue, you can adjust the range to match the number of columns in the `transformedData` object. Here's the modified `appendData` function that takes into account the number of columns in `transformedData`:\n```javascript\nfunction appendData(configByType, configColumns, transformedData) {\n  // 1) Open the spreadsheet and select the sheet\n  const sheet = SpreadsheetApp.openById(configByType.SPREADSHEET_ID).getSheetByName(configByType.SHEET_NAME);\n\n  // 2) Get the starting cell and its row and column numbers\n  const startingCell = sheet.getRange(configByType.LOAD_STARTING_CELL);\n  const startingRow = startingCell.getRow();\n  const startingColumn = startingCell.getColumn();\n\n  // 3) Get existing data from the sheet\n  const lastRow = sheet.getLastRow();\n  const numColumns = transformedData[0].length;\n  const existingData = sheet.getRange(startingRow, startingColumn, lastRow - startingRow + 1, numColumns).getValues();\n\n  // 4) Combine existing data with transformed data\n  const combinedData = existingData.concat(transformedData);\n\n  // 5) Remove duplicates and evaluate rows based on UNIQUE_ID_COLUMN and EVALUATE_COLUMN\n  const uniqueMap = new Map();\n  const filteredData = [];\n\n  combinedData.forEach(row => {\n    const uniqueValue = row[configColumns.UNIQUE_ID_COLUMN-1];\n    const evaluateValue = row[configColumns.EVALUATE_COLUMN-1];\n\n    if (!uniqueMap.has(uniqueValue)) {\n      uniqueMap.set(uniqueValue, row);\n    } else {\n      const existingRow = uniqueMap.get(uniqueValue);\n      const existingEvaluateValue = existingRow[configColumns.EVALUATE_COLUMN-1];\n\n      // Keep the row with the highest value in EVALUATE_COLUMN\n      if (evaluateValue > existingEvaluateValue) {\n        uniqueMap.set(uniqueValue, row);\n      }\n    }\n  });\n\n  // Convert the map values to an array\n  uniqueMap.forEach(row => filteredData.push(row));\n\n  // Sort the filtered data by SORT_COLUMN in descending order\n  filteredData.sort((a, b) => b[configColumns.SORT_COLUMN-1] - a[configColumns.SORT_COLUMN-1]);\n\n  // 6) Clear the existing data range\n  if (lastRow >= startingRow) {\n    sheet.getRange(startingRow, startingColumn, lastRow - startingRow + 1, numColumns).clearContent();\n  }\n\n  // 7) Set the values in the new data range\n  sheet.getRange(startingRow, startingColumn, filteredData.length, numColumns).setValues(filteredData);\n}\n```\nThis modification adjusts the range of `existingData` and the range to clear and set values based on the number of columns in the `transformedData` object. This way, any extra columns after the specified range will be ignored.", 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1246, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Can you make this code better, format it better etc?\n\nimport math\nfrom classes import Appliance\nfrom appliances import nonShiftable, shiftable, auxilary\n\n# \\* Prices generated by generateElectricityPrices.py\nprices = {\n 0: 0.17043824334020852,\n 1: 0.17687964847282173,\n 2: 0.1848716150231699,\n 3: 0.16119955296182936,\n 4: 0.16220396601978404,\n 5: 0.17861609773844922,\n 6: 0.19649085997831328,\n 7: 0.36008332379269636,\n 8: 0.396606737372287,\n 9: 0.5501378614431885,\n 10: 0.5940889787258893,\n 11: 0.18848951740313521,\n 12: 0.18571010112349637,\n 13: 0.16788191713340134,\n 14: 0.1709039894099174,\n 15: 0.16532830213734045,\n 16: 0.4565517132282808,\n 17: 0.5857373298786244,\n 18: 0.3656057042514985,\n 19: 0.49303826836168463,\n 20: 0.38306623023534225,\n 21: 0.43242741485567326,\n 22: 0.1580491724358629,\n 23: 0.17048220206057746\n}\ndef estimate\\_electricity\\_cost\\_for\\_run(hour: int, duration: int, consumes: int) -> int:\n global prices\n cost = 0\n for i in range(hour, hour + duration):\n if i >= 24:\n i -= 24\n cost += prices[i] \\* (consumes / duration)\n return cost\n\n# \\* DESCRIPTION: Estimates the electricity cost for a given hour\n# \\* INPUT: hours: int, min: int, max: int\n# \\* OUTPUT: int\ndef estimate\\_best\\_hour\\_start(duration: int, min\\_hour: int, max\\_hour: int, consumes: int) -> int:\n global prices\n\n min\\_cost = -1\n min\\_index = -1\n if max\\_hour < min\\_hour:\n max\\_hour += 24\n for hour in range(min\\_hour, max\\_hour - duration):\n if hour >= 24:\n hour -= 24\n cost = estimate\\_electricity\\_cost\\_for\\_run(hour, duration, consumes)\n if cost < min\\_cost or min\\_cost == -1:\n min\\_cost = cost\n min\\_index = hour\n\n return min\\_index\n\n# \\* DESCRIPTION: Calculates the optimal schedule for the given appliances\n# \\* INPUT: appliances: Appliance[] -> list of appliances\n# \\* OUTPUT: dict -> {hour: Appliance[]}\ndef optimal\\_calculation(appliances):\n schedule = {}\n for i in range(24):\n schedule[i] = []\n\n # \\* Calculate optimal schedule\n for appliance in appliances:\n if not appliance.shiftable or ((appliance.timeStart + appliance.duration) % 24) == appliance.timeStop % 24:\n schedule[appliance.timeStart].append(appliance)\n continue\n\n hour\\_start = estimate\\_best\\_hour\\_start(\n appliance.duration, appliance.timeStart, appliance.timeStop, appliance.consumption)\n schedule[hour\\_start].append(appliance)\n\n return schedule\n# \\* DESCRIPTION: Calculates total load for each hour based on all appliances, and peak hour + load\n# \\* INPUT: appliances: Appliance[] -> list of appliances\n# \\* OUTPUT: schedule: dict, peakHour: int, peakLoad: float\n\ndef calculate\\_peak\\_load(appliances):\n schedule = {}\n for i in range(24):\n schedule[i] = 0\n # Calculate total energy consumption for all appliances each hour\n for a in appliances:\n if not a.shiftable or ((a.timeStart + a.duration) % 24) == a.timeStop % 24:\n for i in range(24):\n schedule[i] += (a.consumption / 24)/1000\n continue\n hourStart = estimate\\_best\\_hour\\_start(\n a.duration, a.timeStart, a.timeStop, a.consumption\n )\n for i in range(hourStart, (hourStart + a.duration + 1)):\n schedule[i] += (a.consumption / a.duration)/1000\n # Find hour with highest energy consumption\n peakHour = 0\n peakPrice = schedule[peakHour]\n for hour in schedule.keys():\n if schedule[hour] > peakPrice:\n peakHour = hour\n peakPrice = schedule[peakHour]\n\n return schedule, peakHour, peakPrice\ndef scheduleAppliances(appliances):\n schedule = []\n for a in appliances:\n if not a.shiftable or ((a.timeStart + a.duration) % 24) == a.timeStop % 24:\n schedule.append({\n "name": a.name,\n "start": a.timeStart,\n "stop": a.timeStop,\n "duration": a.duration,\n "consumption": (a.consumption/a.duration)\n })\n continue\n optimalStartTime = estimate\\_best\\_hour\\_start(\n a.duration, a.timeStart, a.timeStop, a.consumption\n )\n schedule.append({\n "name": a.name,\n "start": optimalStartTime,\n "stop": a.timeStop,\n "duration": a.duration,\n "consumption": (a.consumption/a.duration)\n })\n\n # Sort schedule by appliance start time\n schedule = sorted(schedule, key=lambda x: x["start"])\n\n return schedule\ndef calculatePeak(schedule):\n hourlyTotalConsumption = {}\n totalCost = 0\n for i in range(24):\n hourlyTotalConsumption[i] = 0\n for appliance in schedule:\n for i in range(appliance["start"], (appliance["start"]+appliance["duration"])):\n hourlyTotalConsumption[i] += appliance["consumption"] / 1000\n peakHour = 0\n peakLoad = hourlyTotalConsumption[peakHour]\n for hour in hourlyTotalConsumption:\n if hourlyTotalConsumption[hour] > peakLoad:\n peakHour = hour\n peakLoad = hourlyTotalConsumption[peakHour]\n\n for x in schedule:\n totalCost += estimate\\_electricity\\_cost\\_for\\_run(\n x["start"], x["duration"], (x["consumption"] \\* x["duration"]) / 1000)\n\n return peakHour, peakLoad, totalCost\ndef applianceReference(appliance):\n for a in nonShiftable:\n if a == appliance["name"]:\n return nonShiftable[a]\n for a in shiftable:\n if a == appliance["name"]:\n return shiftable[a]\n for a in auxilary:\n if a == appliance["name"]:\n return auxilary[a]\ndef optimizeSchedule(schedule):\n # Create copy of schedule\n originalSchedule = schedule.copy()\n peakLoad = calculatePeak(originalSchedule)[1]\n totalCost = calculatePeak(originalSchedule)[2]\n lenght = len(originalSchedule)\n print("Incoming:")\n print("Peak load", peakLoad)\n print("Total cost", totalCost)\n\n for i in range(len(originalSchedule)):\n if originalSchedule[i]["duration"] == 24:\n continue\n appliance = originalSchedule.pop(i)\n ref = applianceReference(appliance)\n for j in range(ref[4], ref[5]-ref[3]):\n originalSchedule.append({\n "name": appliance["name"],\n "start": j,\n "stop": ref[5],\n "duration": ref[3],\n "consumption": appliance["consumption"]\n })\n newPeakLoad = calculatePeak(originalSchedule)[1]\n newTotalCost = calculatePeak(originalSchedule)[2]\n if newPeakLoad > peakLoad and newTotalCost > totalCost:\n del originalSchedule[-1]\n elif newPeakLoad < peakLoad: # her skal det egt stå newPeakLoad < peakLoad AND newTotalCost < total cost, men da kommer det ingen endringer\n peakLoad = newPeakLoad\n totalCost = newTotalCost\n appliance = originalSchedule.pop()\n else:\n del originalSchedule[-1]\n\n if len(originalSchedule) < lenght:\n originalSchedule.append(appliance)\n\n peakLoad = calculatePeak(originalSchedule)[1]\n totalCost = calculatePeak(originalSchedule)[2]\n print("Outgoing:")\n print("Peak load", peakLoad)\n print("Total cost", totalCost)\n\n return originalSchedule\n# \\* DESCRIPTION: Calculates the total daily energy consumption for the given schedule\n# \\* INPUT: schedule: dict -> {hour: Appliance[]}\n# \\* OUTPUT: int\ndef calculate\\_schedule\\_cost(schedule: dict) -> int:\n total = 0\n for hour in schedule:\n for appliance in schedule[hour]:\n total += estimate\\_electricity\\_cost\\_for\\_run(\n hour, appliance.duration, appliance.consumption)\n\n return round(total / 1000, 2)\n\n# \\* DESCRIPTION: Prints the given schedule to the console\n# \\* INPUT: schedule: dict -> {hour: Appliance[]}\n# \\* OUTPUT: None\ndef print\\_schedule(schedule: dict) -> None:\n for hour in schedule.keys():\n if (len(schedule[hour]) == 0):\n continue\n for appliance in schedule[hour]:\n print(\n f\'{f"{hour}:00-{hour + appliance.duration}:00":<11} - {appliance.name:<16} ({appliance.consumption / 1000} kW)\')\ndef print\\_scedule\\_2(schedule):\n totalConsumption = 0\n totalCost = 0\n for x in schedule:\n totalConsumption += (x["consumption"] / 1000) \\* x["duration"]\n totalCost += estimate\\_electricity\\_cost\\_for\\_run(\n x["start"], x["duration"], (x["consumption"] \\* x["duration"])/1000)\n print(x["start"], ":00 -", (x["start"]+x["duration"]),\n ":00 ", x["name"], " - ", (x["consumption"]/1000), "kWh")\n print("Total energy consumption:", round(totalConsumption, 4),\n "kWh\\nTotal energy cost:", round(totalCost/1000, 2), "nok")', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1047, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'The Fresh Earth Ecosystem is a comprehensive food system that aims to transform the agri-food industry. It is designed as a decentralized autonomous organization (DAO) and operates on a token-driven system. The Fresh Earth Token serves as a store of value and a tool for fractional ownership in the food ecosystem business, which also drives the Rewards System, incentivizing participation and engagement from stakeholders. The ecosystem includes a Data Management Platform that measures and validates the impact of regenerative practices based on KPIs such as carbon sequestration, food nutritional density, water retention, etc. The Project Management Platform is a B2B marketplace that brings together stakeholders such as health experts, farm experts, etc., to create and implement high-quality health and farm programs. The Consumer Movement is a digital platform that connects consumers with locally sourced, regenerative food products. The Transport Management System (TMS) optimizes the supply chain and logistics by coordinating local participants in the supply chain. All these components work together to create a sustainable food system that benefits both the environment and consumers.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1438, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': "In this section, several examples are provided to demonstrate the concept of rationalizable choice functions and highlight instances where choice functions cannot be rationalized by preference relations. These examples help to understand the limitations and complexities in human decision-making processes and the challenges in modeling them using preference relations.\n\nExample 2.1 demonstrates a rationalizable choice function for a simple case with three alternatives, while also showing that not all choice functions are rationalizable.\n\nExample 2.2 examines a choice procedure based on the median of an ordered set of alternatives. It shows that this choice function cannot be rationalized by a preference relation but can be rationalized using a different type of rationale.\n\nExample 2.3 explores a situation where an individual's choice changes based on the context or additional information provided, even when the set of alternatives remains the same. This example highlights that sometimes an alternative might be perceived differently in different choice contexts, making the choice function non-rationalizable.\n\nExample 2.4 describes a partygoer's decision-making process that is influenced by social dynamics and not solely based on their preference for the alternatives. This behavior cannot be rationalized by a preference relation over the set of alternatives, as the presence or absence of certain alternatives affects the individual's choice.", 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1303, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Show me how to complete the square. Use Latex to write all of your equations.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1771, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': "Discord offers a range of features that can benefit a community, including:\n\n1. Community servers: Discord provides a platform for various communities, ranging from Harry Potter fans to programming enthusiasts, to connect and build their own servers [[1](https://discord.com/blog/discord-is-for-your-communities)].\n2. Safety measures: Discord has several safety measures in place to ensure that communities remain safe, including requiring members to have a verified email address and enabling Safety Checks Verification Level Setting [[2](https://support.discord.com/hc/en-us/articles/360047132851-Enabling-Your-Community-Server)].\n3. Welcome screens: Users can set up welcome screens to introduce new members to their community and provide them with important information [[3](https://www.howtogeek.com/739355/how-to-set-up-a-community-server-on-discord/)].\n4. Activity detection: Discord allows users to add any running application or game to activity detection, allowing others to see what they are doing [[4](https://www.howtogeek.com/853659/10-discord-features-you-should-be-using/)].\n5. Stage Discovery: This feature allows communities to showcase their events to the global Discord user base and invite potential new members to their community [[5](https://discord.com/blog/whats-next-for-communities-at-discord)].\n6. Forum Channels: Discord's Forum Channels feature allows server owners to create forums within their server and have discussions on various topics [[8](<https://www.theverge.com/2022/9>", 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1251, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': "Here are some plot hooks and rumors for The Discarded Doll's Vindication and Nutmeg's Redemption, each followed by the distorted rumor that may have spread through the game of Telephone:\n\nThe Discarded Doll's Vindication:\n\n1. Plot Hook: A former servant of the Laveau family is seeking help to expose the family's dark secrets.\nRumor: A mysterious stranger is looking for someone to help them bring down a powerful family with a hidden past.\n2. Plot Hook: There's a valuable heirloom hidden in the Laveau estate that could change the family's fortune.\nRumor: There's a hidden treasure in an old mansion that can grant immense wealth to whoever finds it.\n3. Plot Hook: The Laveau family is desperately seeking help to recover from their financial troubles.\nRumor: A noble family is in dire need of assistance to save their crumbling empire.\n\nNutmeg's Redemption:\n\n1. Plot Hook: A magical disturbance in the Murkshire Marshlands could be linked to the hag coven responsible for Nutmeg's curse.\nRumor: Strange magical occurrences in a swampy area might be connected to a group of witches and their dark rituals.\n2. Plot Hook: A cursed individual, similar to Nutmeg, has been sighted in the area, possibly sent to recapture her.\nRumor: A person cursed to become a monstrous creature is on a mission to track down and capture another cursed being.\n3. Plot Hook: An ancient artifact rumored to have the power to break curses has been discovered in a nearby ruin.\nRumor: A powerful item with the ability to undo magical transformations is hidden somewhere in an old, forgotten ruin.\n\nRemember, these distorted rumors can be spread through NPCs or found in various documents, adding layers of intrigue and challenge for the players as they try to uncover the truth behind the rumors.", 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1165, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'You can fix the error by storing the color of the scatter plot in a variable and using that variable to color the regression line. Here is an example of how you can modify the previous code to fix this issue:\n```\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Get the unique participant IDs\nparticipant_ids = data["ppid"].unique()\n\n# Iterate through each participant ID\nfor participant_id in participant_ids:\n    # Filter the data to only include the current participant\'s data\n    participant_data = data[data["ppid"] == participant_id]\n    \n    # Create a new figure for the current participant\n    plt.figure()\n    \n    # Iterate through the conditions\n    for condition in ["0_obj_right", "3_obj_right", "99_obj_right"]:\n        # Filter the data to only include the current condition\'s data\n        condition_data = participant_data[participant_data["trial_name"] == condition]\n        \n        # Plot the data for the current condition and store the color in a variable\n        scatter = plt.scatter(condition_data["trial_num"], condition_data["err_pos_walk"], label=condition)\n        color = scatter.get_facecolors()[0]\n        \n        # Fit the data to a linear function\n        fit = np.polyfit(condition_data["trial_num"], condition_data["err_pos_walk"],1)\n        f = np.poly1d(fit)\n        x = np.linspace(condition_data["trial_num"].min(), condition_data["trial_num"].max(),100)\n        plt.plot(x, f(x), linestyle=\'--\', color=color, alpha = 0.5)\n        \n        #Calculate R-squared\n        y_pred = f(condition_data["trial_num"])\n        y_true = condition_data["err_pos_walk"]\n        R_squared = 1 - (np.sum((y_true - y_pred) ** 2) / np.sum((y_true - np.mean(y_true)) ** 2))\n        plt.text(condition_data["trial_num"].min()+5,condition_data["err_pos_walk"].max()-5,f"R-squared = {R_squared:.4f}")\n        \n    # Set the X and Y axis labels\n    pl\n```', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1086, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Plan de tests pour la fonctionnalité "Récupération des trajets conventionnés dans le bandeau prix" :\n\nPrérequis :\n1. Disposer d\'une base de données contenant des trajets conventionnés dans la table ContractedTrips.\n\nTests de la fonctionnalité :\n\n1. Test de l\'affichage des éléments de l\'interface utilisateur :\na. Vérifier la présence du champ "Rechercher un trajet".\nb. Vérifier la présence du bouton "Lancer la recherche d\'un trajet conventionné" (désactivé tant que le champ "Rechercher un trajet" est vide).\nc. Vérifier la présence du champ "Trajets trouvés (X)" (avec X le nombre de résultats).\n2. Test de la recherche de trajets conventionnés :\na. Saisir une recherche dans le champ "Rechercher un trajet" et vérifier que le bouton de recherche s\'active.\nb. Lancer la recherche et vérifier l\'affichage du chargement.\nc. Vérifier que les résultats de la recherche sont affichés sous forme de liste et correspondent aux critères de recherche (ignorant les majuscules/minuscules, les caractères spéciaux et les accents).\nd. S\'assurer que la recherche prend en compte la coche "Sécurité sociale" pour filtrer les trajets.\ne. Vérifier que le premier trajet de la liste est sélectionné et que les champs "Distance" et "Péage" sont remplis automatiquement.\n3. Test de la recherche avec différents critères :\na. Effectuer des recherches avec des mots clés variés, incluant des noms de villes de départ, d\'arrivée et des établissements de soins.\nb. Vérifier que les résultats correspondent bien aux critères de recherche et que la sélection du trajet met à jour les champs "Distance" et "Péage" en conséquence.\n4. Test de la prise en compte de la coche "SAMU" :\na. Vérifier que le montant du péage est ajusté en fonction de la coche "SAMU" lors de la sélection d\'un résultat.\n5. Test de la recherche avec des mots clés partiellement saisis :\na. Effectuer des recherches avec des mots clés partiellement saisis (par exemple "str col") et vérifier que les résultats correspondent aux trajets attendus ("STRASBOURG > COLMAR").\n\nCe plan de tests permet de valider le bon fonctionnement de la fonctionnalité "Récupération des trajets conventionnés dans le bandeau prix" et de s\'assurer que les critères de recherche sont bien pris en compte.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1082, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Plan de tests pour la fonctionnalité "Ajustements liés aux établissements proposés dans la modale d\'intégration Carius" :\n\nPrérequis :\n1. Disposer d\'une base de données contenant des établissements avec des informations variées (FINESS, RPPS, noms, adresses, etc.).\n2. Avoir des missions avec des informations d\'établissement transmises par le plugin Carius.\n\nTests de la fonctionnalité :\n\n1. Test de l\'affichage de la modale d\'intégration Carius :\na. Vérifier l\'affichage de la modale d\'intégration Carius avec les informations de la mission reçues du plugin.\n2. Test de la recherche d\'établissements basée sur les informations transmises par Carius :\na. Vérifier que la ComboBox propose les établissements trouvés dans la base de données en fonction des informations transmises par Carius (FINESS, RPPS, noms, adresses, etc.).\nb. Vérifier que les établissements sont triés en fonction de leur "note" et que l\'établissement avec la meilleure note est sélectionné par défaut.\n3. Test des différents scénarios de recherche d\'établissements :\na. Si aucun résultat n\'est trouvé, vérifier que la ComboBox n\'est pas affichée et qu\'une checkbox pour créer un établissement temporaire est présente.\nb. Si un résultat est trouvé via le FINESS ou le RPPS, vérifier que la ComboBox ne propose pas l\'option de créer un nouvel établissement.\nc. Si un résultat est trouvé via le nom, vérifier que la ComboBox propose l\'option de créer un nouvel établissement et que les établissements qui matchent le nom et le département du code postal sont affichés dans la ComboBox.\n4. Test de la sélection d\'un établissement :\na. Vérifier que l\'établissement sélectionné est correctement intégré à la mission lors de la validation de la modale.\n5. Test des restrictions de sélection d\'établissements :\na. Si aucun établissement n\'a le même code postal et qu\'il n\'y a pas de match sur le numéro FINESS ni RPPS, vérifier qu\'aucun établissement n\'est sélectionné par défaut et qu\'un message CanExecute est présent pour que l\'utilisateur renseigne l\'établissement.\n\nCe plan de tests permet de valider le bon fonctionnement des ajustements liés aux établissements proposés dans la modale d\'intégration Carius et de s\'assurer que les critères de recherche et de sélection sont bien pris en compte.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1126, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': '1/3 코드 부분\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nimport re\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split, StratifiedKFold, GridSearchCV\nfrom sklearn.metrics import f1_score\nfrom imblearn.over_sampling import SMOTE\nfrom gensim.models import FastText\nfrom nltk.corpus import stopwords\nfrom nltk.tokenize import word_tokenize\nfrom lightgbm import LGBMClassifier\nfrom tqdm.auto import tqdm\nimport torch\nfrom torch.utils.data import Dataset, DataLoader\nfrom transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForSequenceClassification, AdamW, get_linear_schedule_with_warmup\n\n# Data preprocessing\ndef preprocess_text(text):\n    text = re.sub(r"//.*", "", text)\n    text = re.sub(r"http\\S+|www\\S+\n```', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1190, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'According to the provided web search results, Amazon does have an immigration team, called Amazon Immigration, that acts as strategic partners and consultants to business leaders and partner support teams, and centrally manages worldwide immigration filings and the vendors that process them. [[1](https://www.amazon.jobs/en/teams/Global-Immigration)].\nThis team also supports and lobbies for immigration reform, including a legal pathway to citizenship for Dreamers and reforms to the green card and high-skilled visa programs. [[2](https://www.aboutamazon.com/about-us/public-policy/immigration)].\n\nIn addition to this, Amazon has also introduced programs to support and provide resources to refugee and humanitarian-based immigrant employees. The Welcome Door program, which offers additional resources and support along with reimbursement for Employment Authorization Document (EAD) fees and access to upskilling opportunities like free college tuition and ESL proficiency. [[5](https://www.aboutamazon.com/news/workplace/amazon-launches-employment-support-program-for-refugees)]\n\nIt is also seen from the web search results that Amazon employees have had issues with H1B visa transfer and GC EAD in the past, but it is not clear if Amazon has a specific policy for H1B visa transfer or GC EAD. [[7](https://www.teamblind.com/post/Amazon-Immigration-Support-BukidaQc),[8](https://www.teamblind.com/post/Amazon-Immigration-tP4TxL1F)]\n\nIt is also to be noted that there is an increase in the number of Requests for Evidence (RFEs) and Notices of Intent to Deny (NOIDs) issued by U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services in connection to Form I-140, Immigrant Petitions for Alien Worker, (I-140 Petition) filed by U.S. employers on behalf of foreign national employees, which is a required step in the employment-based lawful permanent residence process. [[9](https://www.amazon.com/Immigration-Update-with-Meyner-Landis/dp/B09ZXYYQXL)]\n\nIn conclusion, it is clear that Amazon has an immigration team, called Amazon Immigration, that acts as strategic partners and consultants to business leaders and partner support teams, and centrally manages worldwide immigration filings and the vendors that process them. They also support and lobby for immigration reform, and have programs to support and provide resources to refugee and humanitarian-based immigrant employees. However, it is not clear if Amazon has a specific policy for H1B visa transfer or GC EAD, and it seems that there have been issues with RFEs and NOIDs for foreign national employees in the past.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1157, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
