{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Understanding Guest User Feature\n--------------------------------\n\nCommon Use Cases:\n\n1. Consultants: Companies often invite consultants as guest users when they don\'t want to purchase additional seats for them.\n2. External meetings: Guest users are useful when conducting meetings with prospects or customers.\n3. Client services: Sales representatives, customer success professionals, and pre-sales consultants often invite guest users for collaboration.\n4. Project managers: They frequently use guest users to collaborate and assign tasks across multiple teams.\n\nAdvantages and Limitations:\nGuest users can be helpful for collaborating with external parties, but they can also be confusing. Some of the limitations include:\n\n1. Difficulty in accessing workspaces: If a guest user doesn\'t bookmark their workspace, it can be hard to find it again. They would have to locate the original email invitation, which might have an expired link.\n2. Expiration of links: Access links for guest users can expire between 7 and 14 days, making it difficult for them to log back in.\n3. Renewed access: When a link expires, the guest user needs to request a new link, essentially acting as a refresh token that grants them access for another extended period.\n\nFAQ:\n\nQ: What happens after the 14 days for a guest user? Will they be assigned a new guest account that they can then use to join again, or does it expire permanently?\n\nA: Guest users will receive a new link that acts as a refresh token, giving them renewed access for another extended window.\n\nQ: What is the difference between someone clicking on the "This meeting has notes in Fellow" link and sending the notes to external customers?\n\nA: The link inside the calendar event only works for attendees who are part of the organizing workspace. To invite a guest, the "Share" button should be used, and the workspace needs to have the appropriate settings enabled.\n\nQ: How does a workspace suddenly grow from the original 10 seats to 40 people? Is it because of the access link in the meeting invite or because people are sharing the notes?\n\nA: It is usually a combination of both. The link inside the calendar event is one of the top virality drivers for Fellow, and as users sign up, they are prompted to invite other users as well.\n\nQ: Is it true that there\'s no limit on guest users anymore? Is it unlimited, except only on paid plans?\n\nA: Correct. Guest users are unlimited on Pro and above plans, including the free 14-day trial of Fellow.\n\nUnderstanding Cross Workspace Collaboration Feature\n---------------------------------------------------\n\n(To be continued with the next transcript)\n\nKey Differences Between Guest User and Cross Workspace Collaboration Features\n-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nPricing and plans\n-----------------\n\nTips for Suggesting the Right Feature to Customers\n--------------------------------------------------', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1203, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': "Understanding Cross Workspace Collaboration Feature\n\nCross Workspace Collaboration (XWC) is a new feature that allows different workspaces, even those with the same domain, to collaborate with each other. This can be particularly useful for companies with multiple departments or subsidiaries that want to maintain separate workspaces while still being able to communicate and work together. To use XWC, at least one of the workspaces needs to be on a pro or higher plan. The paid workspace can then invite other workspaces to collaborate, regardless of whether they belong to the same company or not.\n\nKey Differences Between Guest User and Cross Workspace Collaboration Features\n\n1. Domain requirements: Guest users must have a domain external to the account, whereas with XWC, workspaces with the same domain can collaborate.\n2. Gaming the system: With XWC, it's possible for users to game the system by having multiple workspaces with a limited number of users in each, using only one paid workspace to collaborate with the others. However, this behavior will be monitored and controlled on a case-by-case basis.\n3. Expansion possibilities: XWC opens up the opportunity for expansion by allowing a paid workspace for one department to collaborate with other departments that are using a separate, free workspace.\n\nQnA:\n\nQ: Can the same domain be used for Cross Workspace Collaboration?\nA: Yes, workspaces with the same domain can collaborate with each other using Cross Workspace Collaboration.\n\nQ: Is it possible for users to game the system with Cross Workspace Collaboration?\nA: Yes, it is possible for users to game the system by having multiple workspaces with a limited number of users in each and using only one paid workspace to collaborate with the others. However, this behavior will be monitored and controlled on a case-by-case basis.\n\nQ: How can Cross Workspace Collaboration be used as an expansion play?\nA: Cross Workspace Collaboration can be leveraged for expansion by allowing a paid workspace for one department to collaborate with other departments that are using a separate, free workspace. This allows for more efficient collaboration without the need for all departments to pay for additional users.", 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1166, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': "The code provided is a Python binding for the EVA library using the pybind11 library. EVA is a compiler and runtime system for homomorphic evaluation of arithmetic circuits on encrypted data. This binding allows Python users to access EVA's functionalities.\n\nHere's a summary of the code:\n\n1. It defines a constant `SAVE_DOC_STRING` that contains a docstring for serialization-related functions.\n2. It creates a pybind11 module named `_eva` which is a Python wrapper for the EVA library.\n3. The module exposes EVA's core classes and functions to Python, such as `Op`, `Type`, `Term`, `Program`, `evaluate`, and serialization-related functions like `save` and `load`.\n4. It adds a submodule named `_ckks`, which is a Python wrapper for the EVA CKKS compiler. This submodule defines classes like `CKKSCompiler`, `CKKSParameters`, `CKKSSignature`, and `CKKSEncodingInfo`.\n5. Another submodule named `_seal` is added, which is a Python wrapper for the EVA SEAL backend. It defines classes like `SEALValuation`, `SEALPublic`, and `SEALSecret`, and exposes functions like `generateKeys`, `encrypt`, `execute`, and `decrypt`.\n\nThe main purpose of this code is to provide Python bindings for the EVA library, allowing users to work with encrypted data and perform homomorphic evaluations using the CKKS encryption scheme and the SEAL backend.", 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1144, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': "The provided code is a function in a PHP/Laravel application. The function `reCheckPrivateKey` takes two parameters: `$user_encrypted_pk` and `$user_pk`.\n\nIf `$user_encrypted_pk` is not empty, it decrypts the private key using the `decrypt` function provided by Laravel's encryption service, and returns a response with success status, a message indicating the private key was found, and the decrypted private key as the data.\n\nIf `$user_encrypted_pk` is empty, the function checks if `$user_pk` is also empty. If `$user_pk` is empty, it returns a response with error status and a message indicating that the private key is required. If `$user_pk` is not empty, the function returns a response with success status, a message indicating the private key was found, and the value of `$user_pk` as the data.\n\nIt is likely that this function is used to retrieve and validate the private key entered by the user or retrieved from the server. The decryption of `$user_encrypted_pk` assumes that the private key was previously encrypted using the same encryption method and key used by Laravel's encryption service.", 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1559, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'This error indicates that there are some issues with loading the state\\_dict for a PyTorch model. Specifically, there are missing keys and unexpected keys in the state\\_dict, and there are size mismatches for some of the model parameters.\n\nHere are a few things you can try to fix this error:\n\n1. Check the version of PyTorch: Make sure that you are using the same version of PyTorch that was used to train the model. If the version is different, it may cause compatibility issues.\n2. Check the model architecture: Make sure that the architecture of the model in the code is the same as the architecture of the model that was trained. If the architecture is different, the state\\_dict will not be able to load properly.\n3. Check the checkpoint file: Make sure that the checkpoint file contains the correct state\\_dict for the model. If the checkpoint file is corrupted or contains the wrong state\\_dict, it will not be able to load properly.\n4. Try to load the state\\_dict with strict=False: Sometimes, it is possible to load the state\\_dict even if there are missing or unexpected keys, or size mismatches. You can try loading the state\\_dict with the strict=False option, which will ignore these issues and load as much of the state\\_dict as possible. However, this may result in a model that is not exactly the same as the original model.\n\nIf none of these solutions work, you may need to retrain the model with the correct architecture and/or version of PyTorch, or try to obtain a new checkpoint file with the correct state\\_dict.', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1148, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
