{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': "Outcome for the Appellant:\n\n1. The Court ruled that the Builder breached the implied warranties under section 18B(1) of the HBA as the property did not meet the standard of a reasonable person, and awarded the plaintiffs $10,363.65 for breach of warranties.\n2. The Court also ruled that the Builder was liable to pay $152 as liquidated damages to the plaintiffs for breach of contract.\n3. However, the Court allowed the Builder's claim for the outstanding invoice of $81,113.54, and set off the amounts owed to each party.\n4. As a result, the Court ordered the Builder to pay the plaintiffs $70,597.89 in total, after setting off the amounts owed to each party.\n5. The Court further ordered that interest be paid on the amount at court rates from the date of contract termination to the date of judgment, amounting to $5,912.79.\n\nOutcome for the Respondent:\n\n1. The Court rejected the Builder's argument that the defects in the property were caused by the plaintiffs' failure to properly maintain the property after they took possession.\n2. The Court also rejected the Builder's argument that the plaintiffs' claim was barred by the statute of limitations.\n3. However, the Court allowed the Builder's argument that the plaintiffs were not entitled to consequential damages as the contract excluded such damages.\n4. The Court ordered that the proceedings against the second defendant be dismissed, as the plaintiffs' claim against the architect was not proven.\n5. The Court allowed the Builder an opportunity to make written submissions on costs, and directed that the question of costs be determined on the papers.", 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1155, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
{'model': '/data/model/deepseek-aiDeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B', 'prompt': 'Rewrite:\nThe 2023 Israeli judicial reform is a plan to change the legal system in Israel. It aims to reduce the power of the judiciary, particularly the Supreme Court, in making laws and influencing public policy. The reform would let the government control judicial appointments and limit the authority of legal advisors. There is a lot of controversy around this reform, with some people supporting it and others claiming it would give the government too much power.\n\nOutline:\n\n1. Proposed changes to the judicial system\n\t* Limit Supreme Court\'s power to exercise judicial review\n\t* Grant government control over judicial appointments\n\t* Limit the authority of legal advisors\n\tQuote: "It seeks to curb the judiciary\'s intervention and influence over lawmaking and public policy by limiting the Supreme Court\'s power to exercise judicial review, granting the government control over judicial appointments and limiting the authority of its legal advisors."\n2. Reactions to the proposed reform\n\t* Opposition from leaders and activists\n\t* Protests in Israel\n\t* International concern\n\tQuote: "The proposed reform has sparked significant backlash, as well as some support, both inside and outside of Israel."\n3. Background of the Israeli Supreme Court and judicial review\n\t* Current constitutional framework\n\t* Controversy surrounding the court\'s rulings\n\tQuote: "Under Israel\'s current constitutional framework, all legislation, government orders, and administrative actions of state bodies are subject to judicial review by the Supreme Court of Israel."\n4. 1992 Constitutional Revolution and its discontents\n\t* Introduction of human rights to private law\n\t* Legal uncertainty and judicial review according to the judge\'s conscience\n\tQuote: "Between 1992 and 1999, Supreme Court Justice Aharon Barak developed a doctrine in a series of rulings, the guiding principle of which is the introduction of Human rights indirectly to private law."\n5. 2018-2022 Israeli political crisis and 2022 legislative election\n\t* Fifth Knesset election in nearly four years\n\t* Far-right elements gaining power\n\t* Commitment to judicial reform in the incoming government\'s policy guidelines\n\tQuote: "Following the election, Likud leader and former Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu formed the thirty-seventh government of Israel, assigning many powerful positions in the new government to previously marginalized far-right politicians who had long advocated policies that conflicted with many of the supreme court\'s precedents and have sought to curtail its powers."', 'temperature': 0.0, 'best_of': 1, 'max_tokens': 1310, 'logprobs': None, 'stream': True, 'stream_options': {'include_usage': True}}
